# Page Tables

Page tables are the most popular mechanism through which an operating system
provides each process with its own private address space and memory

- Page tables:
  - determine what memory addresses mean
  - determine what parts of physical memory can be accessed
  - allow **isolation** of different process' address spaces
  - allow to **multiplex** processes into a single physical memory
  - allow illusion that each process has full control of memory address space 
    with no other processes able to access
- Page tables provide a level of indirection allowing some tricks used by xv6
  - map same memory (trampoline page) in several address spaces
  - guard kernel and user stacks with unmapped page to detect overflow
  - allocate user heap memory lazily 

# Paging Hardware (1 of 4)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- RISC-V instructions (both user and kernel) manipulate virtual addresses
- The machine's RAM, or physical memory, is indexed with physical addresses
- The RISC-V page table hardware maps each virtual address to physical address
- Xv6 uses RISC-V Sv39 mode
  - only bottom 39 bits (of 64) are used
  - RISC-V page table is array of $2^{27} (134,217,728)$ **page table entries** PTEs
  - Each PTE contains 44-bit physical page number PPN and flags
  - Paging hardware translates top 27 bits of virtual address into page table index to find PTE
  - Results in 56-bit physical address, 44-bits from PPN, and 12-bit offset
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.20]{figures/ch03-fig-3-1-page-table-mapping}
\label{fig:ch03-fig-3-1-page-table-mapping}
\caption{An abstract view of a flat page table mapping virtual to physical addresses \parencite[pg.32]{cox-kaashoek-morris-2026}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::

# Paging Hardware (2 of 4)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.18]{figures/ch03-fig-3-2-riscv-address-translation}
\label{fig:ch03-fig-3-2-riscv-address-translation}
\caption{RISC-V address translation details \parencite[pg.33]{cox-kaashoek-morris-2026}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::: {.column width=60%}
- A page table gives the operating system control over virtual-to-physical
  address translations
  - page size $2^{12} = 4096$ bytes `kernel/riscv.h` `PGSIZE`
  - Such a chunk is called a **page**
- Actual page table layout is a bit more complex, three-level tree
  - root of tree is 4096-byte page table containing 512 PTEs (8 bytes or 64 bits per PTE)
  - first 9 bits of virtual address index top level page table, next mid level, etc.
    - Allows memory-efficient way of managing PTEs
  - Potentially requires 3 memory accesses for each VA $\rightarrow$ PA translation
    - RISC-V CPU uses **Translation Look-aside Buffer** (TLB)
::::
:::

# Paging Hardware (3 of 4)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.18]{figures/ch03-fig-3-2-riscv-address-translation}
\label{fig:ch03-fig-3-2-riscv-address-translation}
\caption{RISC-V address translation details \parencite[pg.33]{cox-kaashoek-morris-2026}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::: {.column width=60%}
- PTE_V Valid, indicates whether the PTE is present, if not reference causes page fault
- PTE_R Readable, controls whether instructions are allowed to read the page
- PTE_W Writable, controls whether instructions are allowed to write to the page
- PTE_X Executable, controls whether CPU may interpret content of page as instructions and execute them
- PTE_U User, controls whether instructions in user mode are allowed to access page
- PTE_A Accessed, indicates if page has ever been accessed (read from) since created
- PTE_D Dirty, indicates if page has been written to
::::
:::

# Paging Hardware (4 of 4)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.18]{figures/ch03-fig-3-2-riscv-address-translation}
\label{fig:ch03-fig-3-2-riscv-address-translation}
\caption{RISC-V address translation details \parencite[pg.33]{cox-kaashoek-morris-2026}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::: {.column width=60%}
- To tell CPU to use page table, kernel writes physical address of root page-table
  into `satp` register 
  - `kernel/start.c` disables paging initially
  - `kernel/main.c` calls `kvminithart()` in `kernel/vm.c` which enables kernel page table
  - Later each process has a page table created, and `satp` is set each time xv6 switches to user mode
- CPU translates all addresses by subsequent instructions using page table
  pointed to by `satp`
  - Each multicore CPU has own `satp` so each can run different processes
    with private address space
- From the kernel's point of view, page table is data stored in memory
  - kernel creates and modifies page tables using code much like any you would
    see for tree-shaped data structure
::::
:::

# Kernel Address Space

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.16]{figures/ch03-fig-3-3-kernel-page-table}
\label{fig:ch03-fig-3-3-kernel-page-table}
\caption{On the left xv6's kernel virtual address space.  RWX refer to PTE read, write and execute permissions.  On the right, the RISC-V physical address space that xv6 expects to see. \parencite[pg.34]{cox-kaashoek-morris-2026}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
- When it starts, xv6 create a single page table describing the kernel's
  address space
  - configures layout of its address space to give it access to all of physical memory
  - configured to allow access to various hardware resources at predictable virtual addresses
  - configured in `kernel/memlayout.h`
::::
:::

# Process Address Space (1 of 2)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.16]{figures/ch03-fig-3-4-process-address-space}
\label{fig:ch03-fig-3-4-process-address-space}
\caption{A process's user address space, with its initial stack \parencite[pg.39]{cox-kaashoek-morris-2026}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::: {.column width=60%}
- Each process has its own page table
- When xv6 switches between processes, it also changes page tables
- User addresses start at 0 and end at MAXVA (0x4000000000) `kernel/riscv.h` near bottom
- A processes address space has pages with
  - text of the program (instructions, permissions are PTE_R, PTE_X and PTE_U)
  - pre-initialized data (constants, global variables PTE_R, PTE_W, PTE_U)
  - a page for the stack (PTE_R, PTE_W, PTE_U)
  - pages for dynamic heap (PTE_R, PTE_W, PTE_U)
::::
:::

# Process Address Space (2 of 2)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.16]{figures/ch03-fig-3-4-process-address-space}
\label{fig:ch03-fig-3-4-process-address-space}
\caption{A process's user address space, with its initial stack \parencite[pg.39]{cox-kaashoek-morris-2026}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::: {.column width=60%}
Some properties of xv6 use of page tables here:

1. Different process' page tables translate user addresses to different pages
   of physical memory, so that each process has private user memory
2. Each process sees its memory as having contiguous virtual addresses starting
   at 0, while the process's physical memory can be non-contiguous
3. The kernel maps a page with trampoline code at the top of the user address space 
   (without PTE_U), thus a single page of physical memory shows up in all
   address spaces, but can be used only by the kernel.
::::
:::



